{"reply":"Yes. In **Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,”** the gardeners explain that the wrong tree was planted “by mistake” and that they are painting it because, “if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.” The mistake is being repaired, but under the pressure of anticipated punishment, concealment becomes part of the repair.

Your note has a similar tension: the billing mistake was fixed, but you said nothing afterward. A 2023 study of hospital nurses found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety affected people’s willingness to report errors, while fear was a recurring barrier ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)). That research offers a lens, not a diagnosis of your motive: it suggests that silence can reflect perceived consequences, not necessarily indifference or bad character.

